🧩 Feature Details

1. Registration

Users can create an account using:

Username

Email

Password

Password confirmation

The registration form checks that the email address is not already registered.

The application uses Django's built-in UserCreationForm, providing Django's standard password validation behavior.

2. Login

Users authenticate using:

Username
Password

Successful authentication creates a Django session and redirects the user to the dashboard.

Invalid credentials produce an error message.

3. Logout

Authenticated users can log out through the account logout route.

The Django session is terminated and the user is redirected to the login page.

4. Profile Management

Users can update:

Username

Email

First name

Last name

The profile page uses Django forms and Bootstrap styling.

5. Password Change

Users can change their password using the current password and a new password.

The application uses:

update_session_auth_hash(request, user)

This keeps the current authenticated session valid after a successful password change.

🔗 URL Shortening

Short ID

The current implementation creates a short identifier using a UUID-derived value for the create operation.

The Url model also contains a save-time fallback that generates a UUID-based identifier when no identifier is supplied.

The short identifier is unique at the database level:

uuid = models.CharField(max_length=10, unique=True, blank=True)

Production recommendation: use a dedicated collision-resistant generator with retry logic or a deterministic encoding strategy for very high-volume systems.

🗃️ Database Design

The application primarily uses two models.

Url

Field

Type

Purpose

link

URLField

Original destination URL

uuid

CharField

Unique short identifier

user

ForeignKey

Owner of the URL

click_count

PositiveIntegerField

Total redirect count

is_active

BooleanField

Enables/disables redirect

created_at

DateTimeField

Creation time

Relationship:

User
  │
  └───< Url
           │
           └───< UrlClick

UrlClick

Field

Type

Purpose

url

ForeignKey

Related shortened URL

ip_address

GenericIPAddressField

Visitor IP

user_agent

TextField

Raw browser User-Agent

platform

CharField

Operating-system family

browser

CharField

Browser family

device

CharField

Device family

created_at

DateTimeField

Click timestamp

Because UrlClick uses a foreign key to Url, deleting a URL also deletes its associated click records.

🔄 URL Redirect Workflow

The redirect route follows this logical sequence:

Visitor
   │
   ▼
/<short-id>/
   │
   ▼
Find active Url
   │
   ├── Not found → 404
   │
   ▼
Increment click_count
   │
   ▼
Read User-Agent
   │
   ▼
Detect platform/browser/device
   │
   ▼
Store UrlClick
   │
   ▼
Redirect
   │
   ▼
Original URL

The click counter uses Django's F() expression:

Url.objects.filter(pk=url_obj.pk).update(
    click_count=F('click_count') + 1
)

This is preferable to a simple read-modify-write operation because the database performs the increment atomically.

📊 Click Analytics

The click-history page displays records associated with a user's URL.

A click can contain:

IP Address
Browser
Platform
Device
Date/Time

This provides basic traffic intelligence without requiring a separate analytics platform.

Possible uses

Identify browser distribution

Identify desktop/mobile traffic

Investigate suspicious traffic patterns

Understand URL usage

Audit individual redirects

Review click activity over time

Privacy note: IP addresses and User-Agent information can be personal or potentially identifying data. Production deployments should define an appropriate retention policy, privacy notice, access controls, and legal basis for collection.

🖥️ Dashboard

The dashboard is available to authenticated users.

It calculates:

Total URLs
Total Clicks
User-owned URL list

Short URLs are converted into absolute URLs using the current request host.

Example:

https://your-domain.com/Ab12x9/

Users can manage their own URLs from the dashboard.

⚡ AJAX URL Management

The project supports asynchronous operations for URL management.

Create

POST /create/

Returns JSON containing information such as:

{
    "id": 1,
    "link": "https://example.com",
    "click_count": 0,
    "created_at": "2026-09-01 12:00:00",
    "full_short_url": "http://127.0.0.1:8000/abc123"
}

Edit

POST /edit/<id>/

Updates the user's own URL.

Delete

POST /delete/<id>/

Deletes the user's own URL.

Advantages of AJAX

No complete page refresh

Faster user experience

Dynamic dashboard operations

Easier inline management

JSON responses suitable for JavaScript UI updates

🔐 Ownership and Authorization

URL modification views use:

get_object_or_404(
    Url,
    id=id,
    user=request.user
)

This means a logged-in user can only edit/delete URLs belonging to that user.

Click-history access follows the same ownership model.

This is an important protection against insecure direct object reference (IDOR) vulnerabilities.

